Consider an artificial intelligence system that uses logistic regression to learn an image classifier. Logistic regression amounts to finding the minimum of a differentiable convex objective function, the empirical risk \[ f({\mathbf w}) :=  rac{1}{m} \sum_{r=1}^{m} L\left({\mathbf z}^{(r)},{\mathbf w} ight) 	ext{.} \] Here, L(z(r),w ) denotes the loss incurred on the data point z(r), a feature vector together with its label, of a training set D(train) = z(1), …, z(m) by the hypothesis with model parameters w. The number of data points in the training set is m. Gradient descent searches for a minimum of a differentiable convex function by repeating the gradient step \[ {\mathbf w}^{(t+1)} = {\mathbf w}^{(t)} - \eta^{(t)} 
abla f({\mathbf w}^{(t)}) 	ext{,} \] with a step size η(t) > 0 that is small enough for each step to decrease f (see gradient descent). The gradient of the above objective function is ∇ f(w) = (1)/(m) Σr=1m ∇w L(z(r),w ), an average with one term per data point. Its exact evaluation therefore requires a pass over the entire training set, which is computationally expensive when m is large. SGD instead uses the cheaper estimate \[ g({\mathbf w}) :=  rac{1}{B} \sum_{r \in \mathcal{B}} 
abla_{{\mathbf w}} L\left({\mathbf z}^{(r)},{\mathbf w} ight) 	ext{,} \] the same average over a batch B ⊆ 1, …, m of B = |B| indices chosen uniformly at random (see Fig.~1). Each SGD iteration generates a new batch B(t) and performs the gradient descent update with the estimate in place of the gradient (see Fig.~2), \[ {\mathbf w}^{(t+1)} = {\mathbf w}^{(t)} - \eta^{(t)}  rac{1}{B} \sum_{r \in \mathcal{B}^{(t)}} 
abla_{{\mathbf w}} L\left({\mathbf z}^{(r)},{\mathbf w}^{(t)} ight) 	ext{,} \] with a step size η(t) > 0. The update is a random instance of the gradient descent operator. For a uniformly drawn batch, the expectation of the update is the gradient descent step w ↦ w - η(t) ∇ f(w). The fixed points of that step are the points satisfying the zero-gradient condition ∇ f(w) = 0. One classical set of sufficient conditions for the convergence of such random iterations to a minimum of f comes from stochastic approximation. The step sizes must diminish such that Σt=1∞ η(t) = ∞ while Σt=1∞ (η(t))2 < ∞. The batch size B is an important parameter of SGD: B = m recovers gradient descent, while B = 1 updates with the gradient of a single data point and yields the noisiest estimate. SGD with 1 < B < m is mini-batch SGD. Each update costs B gradient evaluations instead of m. This is what makes empirical risk minimization on large training sets feasible, the main application of SGD in machine learning. The update is noisier than a gradient step, and its variance shrinks as the batch grows. A larger batch draws the dashed curve of Fig.~2 closer to the solid one. At B = m the two coincide; at B = 1 they lie furthest apart. When the training set is spread over several servers, as in Fig.~1, a batch can be drawn from one server at a time, so no server needs the whole training set. See also: gradient descent, gradient-based method, gradient, empirical risk minimization, batch, step size, objective function, zero-gradient condition, online gradient descent.
